# koto artifact manifest — the trust anchor for `make fetch`.
#
# Plain sha256sum(1) format over the artifacts AS INSTALLED, relative to the
# repo root: `make fetch` checks what it decompressed against it, in a staging
# dir, before anything moves into the tree.
#
# This file is COMMITTED ON PURPOSE. It is what makes `make fetch` safe: the
# checksums arrive over git, not over the same connection as the artifacts
# they vouch for, so a compromised mirror cannot vouch for itself.
#
# It vouches for the DOWNLOAD route only. The builds are not reproducible (koto
# stamps `git describe`; vmlinux and rootfs.img embed build timestamps and
# resolved package versions), so `make build` output will not match these
# lines, and nothing holds it to them — `make install` checks presence only.
#
# Entries look like:
#   <sha256>  koto
#   <sha256>  koto-tui
#   <sha256>  fcassets/firecracker
#   <sha256>  fcassets/vmlinux
#   <sha256>  fcassets/rootfs.img
#
# Empty until the first release is published. Until then DIST_VERSION in the
# Makefile reads "unreleased" and `make fetch` says so rather than 404ing.
#
# `make dist` regenerates the entries below from the build it packages.
7884cdeb6b1b634dc593f5ec7171fd6a301800f7c3fb491e4fc70ad1e52fce65  koto
a0a828e38f66c044a51eeee08e3adf7e5a8e26796366e3e07c73857e2e86a405  koto-tui
98ad7b3a21255fb9227dfe564a8c3e170d9272a6b1ac726a0e2f10a2d050e678  fcassets/firecracker
a5b340a31ee7528e66f5d75dfc41938704910ab1ee5ffcb97c733853b6c5f642  fcassets/vmlinux
172f44875a4807a984bc1468f65f79f156aa92222590d7699c7410b7410c73b2  fcassets/rootfs.img
